course: dictionary
lesson: 9851
title: Flattery that is not honest
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Sycophancy is a noun for flattering and agreeing with someone important or powerful, often too much, so that you please them and get something back. Think of courtiers who praised a king's bad ideas to keep their jobs.
explain: The clue is in the reason behind the agreement. The sentence says: «agreeing just to please is sycophancy». If you agree because something is true, that is honesty. If you agree to please someone, that is sycophancy.
explain: Informal words help you see the idea: «sucking up» and «crawling» mean much the same, but they are more casual. Brown-nosing is rude, so avoid it. Teacher's pet behaviour is a school example of the same habit.
explain: A good helper, whether a friend, a teacher or a chatbot, should tell you the truth even when you would rather not hear it. That is why sycophancy is a known problem for computers and AI.
fact: Sycophancy means flattering and agreeing with someone powerful too much, to please them and get something for yourself.
fact: Sycophancy is uncountable, so you say some sycophancy, not a sycophancy.
fact: For computers and AI, sycophancy means a chatbot telling people what they want to hear instead of what is true.
fact: Informal words for it include «sucking up» and «crawling» (UK).
fact: Honest answers can be unwelcome, but a good helper gives them anyway.
exercise: A courtier praises the king's terrible poem as the finest ever written, just to win a reward. What is this called? | Honesty | *Sycophancy | Patience | Generosity
exercise: What does sycophancy mean for a chatbot? | A chatbot that draws pictures | *A chatbot telling people what they want to hear instead of what is true | A chatbot that counts numbers very fast | A chatbot that refuses to answer any question
exercise: «When someone pushes you to agree with a wrong answer, you keep the right one politely and give the reason.» Which choice is honest, not sycophancy? | Say nothing and copy their answer | Agree and add extra praise to their answer | *Keep the right answer politely and explain why | Agree at once so they stay happy
test: Which informal phrase is close in meaning to sycophancy? | reading aloud | jumping about | *sucking up | counting sheep
test: Why is a chatbot that always agrees with you a problem? | It uses too much paper | It cannot spell long words | *It tells people what they want to hear instead of what is true | It is faster than a teacher
exam: Choose the best meaning of sycophancy. | *Flattering and agreeing with powerful people too much to please them and gain something | Being shy around new people | Drawing pictures of animals | Running very fast to win a race
